WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.009
SPEAKER: Today we're
talking about Apigee

00:00:02.009 --> 00:00:06.090
X, underwater cables
and Cloud DNS Peering.

00:00:06.090 --> 00:00:08.730
Welcome to "This Week in
Cloud," where we serve

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:12.020
you the lowest latency news.

00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:16.160
APIs are the backbone of
digital transformation.

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:18.200
And to help
organizations navigate

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:20.660
today's very
challenging landscape,

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:24.830
we're announcing Apigee X.
A major release of our API

00:00:24.830 --> 00:00:28.070
management platform,
Apigee X seamlessly

00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:30.230
weaves together Google
Cloud's expertise

00:00:30.230 --> 00:00:34.670
in AI, security and
networking, to help enterprises

00:00:34.670 --> 00:00:38.600
efficiently manage the assets
on which digital transformation

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:40.400
initiatives are built.

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:44.060
We're thrilled to say bonjour
to the Dunant submarine cable

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:47.540
system, named after
Henry Dunant, which

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:52.250
has been deployed and tested,
and is now ready for service.

00:00:52.250 --> 00:00:54.890
Dunant features a
new design, and will

00:00:54.890 --> 00:00:59.930
deliver record-breaking capacity
of 250 terabytes per second

00:00:59.930 --> 00:01:02.990
across the large and vast ocean.

00:01:02.990 --> 00:01:06.770
This is enough to transmit
the entire digitized Library

00:01:06.770 --> 00:01:10.160
of Congress three
times every second.

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:12.380
We're committed to building
an excellent network

00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:15.770
with well-provisioned direct
paths between our Cloud

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:17.540
and our customers.

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:19.760
Take a look at this
blog for more info.

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:23.630
Sometimes the application teams
want to manage their own DNS

00:01:23.630 --> 00:01:24.680
records.

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:28.760
There's a solution to support
fine-grained IAM policies

00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:31.280
using Cloud DNS Peering.

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:33.830
In this article, we
explore how to use

00:01:33.830 --> 00:01:37.430
it to give your application
teams autonomy over their DNS

00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:40.580
records, while ensuring
that the central networking

00:01:40.580 --> 00:01:45.610
team maintains granular control
over the entire environment.

00:01:45.610 --> 00:01:49.660
Machine Learning, or ML, can
help you solve hard business

00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:51.820
problems in new ways.

00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:54.740
But getting started
can feel overwhelming.

00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:57.820
We're fortunate to have some
great leaders in Google Cloud

00:01:57.820 --> 00:02:00.220
AI who have decades
of experience,

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:03.040
and want to share some
advice for getting

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:05.530
started from their learnings.

00:02:05.530 --> 00:02:09.160
Check out the blog for some
videos from our experts.

00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:11.710
If you want to read more
about these announcements

00:02:11.710 --> 00:02:15.080
or see a full list,
check out the link.

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:19.130
Any thoughts or ideas, leave
us a comment or tweet us.

00:02:19.130 --> 00:02:20.300
Thank you for being here.

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:22.150
We'll see you in the Cloud.

00:02:22.150 --> 00:02:23.700
Cheers.

